  "question": {
    "text": "Metals generally melt at very high temperatures, Among the following which one has the highest melting point?"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Transition metals have high melting point due to their high enthalpy of atomisation. They form strong metallic bonds with unpaired electrons.</p><p>Melting point of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>961</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p><p>Melting point of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>28</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p><p>Melting point of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ga</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p><p>Melting point of&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>38</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>83</mn><mo>&#176;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Cs</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Ga</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Hg</mi></math>&nbsp;are liquid at or near room temperature.</p><p>Among the given options&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ag</mi></math>&nbsp;has the maximum Melting point, as it belongs to&nbsp;transition&nbsp;metal series.</p>"
  }
